Implementation notes:
A connection is TCP on the loopback address; 
a Message and its Answer are UTF-8 text.//No? what does it even means, they are sent on the shell and on system.out, so it depends on how the OS want to encode them, right?
The first line fearlessMessage sends on a connection is the base16 of the 'manager path', and fearlessManager answers that line only when that line is the base16 of its own 'manager path'.
//This seems to be something that should be in the stories, in particular, what happens if it is not the expected base16?
When no answer to that first line arrives within a timeout, the process on the port X is not the 'single' manager: fearlessMessage ends the connection and calls fearlessMessage$send(Message) again.
//So that we can go in loop if there is something planted on that port? I'm not sure this is working.
fearlessManager runs in a process of its own, independent of fearlessMessage: fearlessManager does not keep the standard output and the standard error of fearlessMessage, and fearlessMessage reads text from the standard error of fearlessManager.
The bytes of a Base16 path: on Windows the UTF-16 code units of the name, little endian (a name may contain an unpaired surrogate); elsewhere the bytes of the name as the file system stores them.
